WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.788 --> 00:00:03.591
which is a humanitarian aid for the Moscow
office.

00:00:03.611 --> 00:00:04.993
I will show you how it works.

00:00:04.994 --> 00:00:06.334
I will show you the video.

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:22.534
There's a lot of very brave and
extraordinary work that goes on around the

00:00:22.554 --> 00:00:25.276
world from people who the public don't
really know very much about.

00:00:25.516 --> 00:00:30.019
Politicians and society tends to be rather
cynical about journalism, but I see

00:00:30.239 --> 00:00:32.360
enormous bravery, enormous challenge,

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.822
great courage going on to surface things
that the public really needs to know

00:00:35.862 --> 00:00:36.562
about.

00:00:41.848 --> 00:00:44.850
Journalists go towards danger when others
move away.

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:49.333
Journalists have to get involved with all
sides of the conflict in order to get the

00:00:49.353 --> 00:00:50.053
story.

00:01:25.493 --> 00:01:29.774
It takes a very fearless journalist to get
up every day and say, you know what, I'm

00:01:29.794 --> 00:01:32.395
determined to continue my profession, even
though I

00:01:32.396 --> 00:01:34.696
may have to spend the rest of my life
looking over my shoulder.

00:02:16.857 --> 00:02:17.557
I

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.001
I came here in 2003.

00:02:44.722 --> 00:02:46.423
I came to cover the US-led invasion.

00:02:46.943 --> 00:02:52.127
I got here about three weeks before the
bombing campaign started and I stayed for

00:02:52.128 --> 00:02:53.808
the next couple of years working on and
off.

00:02:54.748 --> 00:02:58.792
I came because I thought the war was going
to to be covered pretty heavily in the

00:02:59.133 --> 00:03:00.995
Western media from the

00:03:01.716 --> 00:03:03.318
position of embedded journalists.

00:03:11.908 --> 00:03:12.908
going to take any risk at all.

00:03:12.928 --> 00:03:15.790
I'd rather take the risk making a story
that wasn't being told.

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:20.272
And I was concerned that the Iraqi
perspective would be neglected.

00:03:37.904 --> 00:03:42.365
So I came and stayed in Baghdad and worked
in Baghdad instead of working embedded

00:03:42.366 --> 00:03:43.225
with the US military.

00:03:45.306 --> 00:03:47.747
It used to be that you could do a mix of
both.

00:03:47.947 --> 00:03:53.188
I tried to keep it 50-50 so that I saw the
war from the soldiers' point of view and I

00:03:53.228 --> 00:03:54.889
saw the war from the

00:03:55.149 --> 00:03:56.529
local Iraqis' point of view.

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:01.470
Journalists play this vital role as sort
of an outside presence, an independent

00:04:01.931 --> 00:04:04.951
person to sort of see how this war is
being conducted.

00:04:13.987 --> 00:04:18.992
If you want to get to some of these
stories, you have to take the risks of the

00:04:19.032 --> 00:04:20.894
people who are living through

00:04:21.054 --> 00:04:21.855
the same thing.

00:04:22.155 --> 00:04:27.240
And sometimes that means risking torture,
risking kidnapping, risking death.

00:04:28.181 --> 00:04:28.881
I faced it.

00:04:28.942 --> 00:04:30.464
Tremendous amount of danger.

00:04:31.105 --> 00:04:32.486
I was shot at several times.

00:04:32.546 --> 00:04:36.471
My car still bears the marks of bullets.

00:04:36.972 --> 00:04:38.734
I was chased several times.

00:04:38.834 --> 00:04:44.441
I got messages, letters taped on the
outdoor of my house.

00:04:48.156 --> 00:04:54.810
As the militants went from using us to
reach the Western audience to just simply

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:59.639
lumping us in with troops and foreigners
as the enemy, we had to start...

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:02.622
Bedding with the troops, that was the
safest option.

00:05:03.263 --> 00:05:07.847
That option that used to be viewed as most
dangerous was now the least worst option.

00:05:08.627 --> 00:05:12.891
But when you travel with troops, you face
the same risks.

00:05:14.853 --> 00:05:18.116
We wanted to go out with troops on
Memorial Day.

00:05:19.136 --> 00:05:24.681
This was four months after ABC News anchor
Bob Woodruff had been hit by an improvised

00:05:24.701 --> 00:05:26.102
explosive device, and he was still

00:05:26.143 --> 00:05:27.604
recuperating from a brain injury.

00:05:28.684 --> 00:05:34.968
What was supposed to be a routine shoot
lasting a couple of hours ended in

00:05:35.188 --> 00:05:35.888
horrible

00:05:35.888 --> 00:05:36.388
tragedy.

00:05:42.452 --> 00:05:49.316
We ended up parked on a residential side
street, dusty, quiet, just about

00:05:49.356 --> 00:05:52.258
deserted, and we got out of our vehicles.

00:05:52.938 --> 00:05:56.320
Paul Douglas and James Brolin were filming
in our direction.

00:05:58.584 --> 00:06:00.065
Turns out the insurgents were watching.

00:06:03.687 --> 00:06:09.010
They command detonated an approximately
500 pound car bomb through all of us.

00:06:11.671 --> 00:06:14.473
It killed James, our sound man, instantly.

00:06:15.393 --> 00:06:17.354
Left Paul fighting for his life.

00:06:18.435 --> 00:06:19.255
He did not make it.

00:06:19.656 --> 00:06:21.016
He died at the scene.

00:06:21.196 --> 00:06:23.338
Killed the captain and his translator
instantly.

00:06:24.298 --> 00:06:27.380
Left me fighting for my life along with
four other.

00:06:27.804 --> 00:06:28.504
Soldiers.

00:06:30.005 --> 00:06:32.947
I technically died five times on the
operating table.

00:06:33.127 --> 00:06:37.709
I had both femurs shattered, shrapnel to
the brain, both eardrums blown out.

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:44.333
I was burned from my hips to my ankles,
but worst of all my femoral artery was

00:06:44.413 --> 00:06:45.113
cut.

00:06:45.514 --> 00:06:48.575
So I bled out more than half my blood they
estimate.

00:06:50.016 --> 00:06:53.138
The other folks with me were equally
horribly injured.

00:06:53.758 --> 00:06:57.040
So it was a handful.

00:06:57.348 --> 00:07:00.769
For the surgeons to try to salvage.

00:07:10.712 --> 00:07:14.693
Kidnapping was and is a very real problem
in Iraq.

00:07:15.074 --> 00:07:19.755
Criminal gangs, militias are roaming
around, finding ways to extort money.

00:07:19.915 --> 00:07:22.736
This is why we've had doctors, academics,
lawyers.

00:07:23.557 --> 00:07:27.521
a whole lot of Iraq's intelligentsia just
flee the country because they're at grave

00:07:27.581 --> 00:07:30.404
risk of being kidnapped.

00:07:30.444 --> 00:07:35.148
Anyone is at risk who can fetch a better
ransom.

00:07:36.269 --> 00:07:40.994
You have to be thinking, okay, it doesn't
matter that I'm a reporter.

00:07:41.355 --> 00:07:46.540
Now to them, I am a Westerner with a
potential big price tag on my head.

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:09.905
We went to do a report with Hussein, the
driver, at the University of Baghdad,

00:08:10.046 --> 00:08:10.746
where the

00:08:10.746 --> 00:08:14.790
residents of a city were being bombarded
by the US, the city of Fallujah.

00:08:14.791 --> 00:08:21.256
We did our interview, we got into a car,
we got out of the

00:08:21.316 --> 00:08:22.016
university.

00:08:22.698 --> 00:08:27.643
When another car stopped us, and some guys
with Kalashnikovs got us out of the car...

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:36.341
It's true that you really feel like you've
passed the other side of the mirror.

00:08:36.342 --> 00:08:40.723
I remember I was in this car that was
taking us, so we were taken off.

00:08:40.763 --> 00:08:45.044
And I saw through the window the life that
was going on for people.

00:08:45.045 --> 00:08:50.365
People in the cars next door, with a guy
smoking, his wife, people putting on the

00:08:50.485 --> 00:08:51.185
radio.

00:08:51.185 --> 00:08:52.326
There is a lot of bottling in Baghdad, so
we were doing well.

00:08:52.327 --> 00:08:56.047
And I knew that I was already in this car,
in another world.

00:08:56.347 --> 00:08:58.628
It was over, there was no way to stop it.

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:04.403
It's even weirder because there are a lot
of checkpoints.

00:09:04.563 --> 00:09:10.108
We passed a checkpoint and I was

00:09:14.031 --> 00:09:20.376
like, Hey, you, get down so we don't see
you.

00:09:20.396 --> 00:09:22.238
We crossed the

00:09:22.278 --> 00:09:27.422
mirror.

00:09:31.276 --> 00:09:32.597
I'm a journalist in Liberation.

00:09:34.219 --> 00:09:35.660
Please, help me.

00:09:35.661 --> 00:09:37.561
My health is very...

00:09:37.562 --> 00:09:39.223
Negotiations with hostages are happening.

00:09:39.224 --> 00:09:40.964
We were locked in a cell, we didn't see
anyone.

00:09:40.965 --> 00:09:46.068
We were in the dark, we had very little to
eat, we didn't wash.

00:09:46.349 --> 00:09:48.330
So we had an animal life.

00:09:48.331 --> 00:09:51.573
We were chained on the ground, so it was
really that life.

00:09:51.574 --> 00:09:54.555
I ask especially Mr Didier Julia, the
French deputy.

00:09:55.096 --> 00:09:57.638
Please Mr Julia, help me, it's urgent.

00:09:57.998 --> 00:09:59.720
When I was a hostage in the...

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.801
In the place where we were detained, there
were 13 other people who were not

00:10:02.821 --> 00:10:03.521
journalists.

00:10:03.522 --> 00:10:05.122
There was one journalist for 13 citizens.

00:10:05.703 --> 00:10:09.784
We are never the only apparent face, I was
going to say, of a repression that

00:10:09.904 --> 00:10:11.745
generally affects everyone when it comes
on us.

00:10:28.036 --> 00:10:31.557
You know, as dangerous as it might seem
sometimes in a place like that, you know,

00:10:31.617 --> 00:10:34.798
it's unstable where the government's
really out of control or

00:10:34.838 --> 00:10:37.959
there's a lot of fighting and insecurity.

00:10:38.219 --> 00:10:43.321
If you make sort of friends who can show
you the way and give you some support

00:10:43.621 --> 00:10:45.022
locally, then it's

00:10:45.102 --> 00:10:46.302
not as bad as it seems.

00:10:47.423 --> 00:10:50.904
In fact, I often felt like I was sort of
protected by the people around me as best

00:10:50.924 --> 00:10:51.624
as anyone could be.

00:11:04.334 --> 00:11:07.856
The basic rule is that you are only as
good as the local journalist you're

00:11:07.857 --> 00:11:08.557
working with.

00:11:08.837 --> 00:11:12.059
They are by far the most important
decision you'll make.

00:11:12.519 --> 00:11:15.521
They understand what's happening on the
ground in a way you never can.

00:11:17.502 --> 00:11:20.864
If you wanted to be a good journalist in
Iraq, you had to risk your life.

00:11:21.805 --> 00:11:22.885
Other way you can't do it.

00:11:23.666 --> 00:11:26.508
We risked our life to get the story, and
it was worth it.

00:11:29.568 --> 00:11:32.550
I really believe there's something called
ground truth that journalists get when

00:11:32.551 --> 00:11:36.293
they go into a place, talk to local
people, and they see with their own eyes

00:11:36.333 --> 00:11:37.033
what is

00:11:37.033 --> 00:11:38.914
happening, and there are facts that they
can convey.

00:11:39.315 --> 00:11:43.197
As you may have heard, a Reuters
television sound man was killed today

00:11:43.818 --> 00:11:44.859
while driving in Baghdad.

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:45.759
He was killed by U.S.

00:11:45.819 --> 00:11:47.500
soldiers, and a cameraman was wounded.

00:11:48.201 --> 00:11:50.002
Unfortunately, this kind of thing is
alarmingly common.

00:11:51.203 --> 00:11:55.586
The Geneva Convention, of course, means
that civilians can't be targeted, and

00:11:55.606 --> 00:11:57.387
journalists are, by definition, civilians.

00:12:05.308 --> 00:12:06.369
We have had people killed.

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:10.214
It is really because the military says
they cannot tell the difference of a

00:12:10.254 --> 00:12:13.598
journalist doing his work on the
battlefield and someone who is on the

00:12:13.599 --> 00:12:15.720
battlefield for nefarious reasons.

00:12:16.060 --> 00:12:19.745
And so then the Geneva Convention for all
the protections that it grants civilians

00:12:20.025 --> 00:12:22.288
does not help the journalist in the field.

00:12:57.448 --> 00:13:00.794
I do not believe that the military is
anti-journalist.

00:13:01.314 --> 00:13:06.002
I do believe that the military is facing a
very different kind of battlefield, where

00:13:06.003 --> 00:13:07.885
you don't know who's a friend and who's an
enemy.

00:13:08.265 --> 00:13:11.470
It's a very frightening place for
journalists and for soldiers.

00:13:13.576 --> 00:13:17.740
About one third of the journalists who get
killed, which is about a thousand over the

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:20.563
last two decades, one third of them are
killed in

00:13:20.603 --> 00:13:21.303
armed conflict.

00:13:21.664 --> 00:13:24.987
The actual problem in terms of numbers is
really on the local level.

00:13:25.748 --> 00:13:28.931
That's where most of the journalists get
killed, not foreign correspondents, local

00:13:28.971 --> 00:13:29.671
journalists.

00:13:47.559 --> 00:13:51.081
Particularly in the case of Iraq, we've
seen more of our colleagues locally

00:13:51.201 --> 00:13:51.901
killed.

00:13:53.123 --> 00:13:53.823
They live there.

00:13:53.883 --> 00:13:56.385
The front line, if you like, is outside
their front door.

00:14:26.024 --> 00:14:28.545
AP doesn't have people parachuting in from
outside.

00:14:28.845 --> 00:14:30.766
We have people who live and work in those
countries.

00:14:31.306 --> 00:14:36.108
We feel that it's important to have folks
who live in a place explain it to the

00:14:36.109 --> 00:14:36.809
world.

00:14:37.269 --> 00:14:42.171
In April of 2004, Bilal Hussein, a
photographer for the Associated Press in

00:14:42.231 --> 00:14:44.072
Fallujah, was picked up by the

00:14:44.073 --> 00:14:50.714
military, which believed that he was
working for insurgents and held him for

00:14:51.155 --> 00:14:53.916
what ultimately was two years without
charges.

00:15:02.976 --> 00:15:07.397
During the US Marines siege of Fallujah as
they were trying to drive the insurgents

00:15:07.477 --> 00:15:09.918
out of Bilal State, it's

00:15:09.919 --> 00:15:15.320
his native city, he took a lot of pictures
of insurgents fighting and lots of other

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:16.881
scenes of fighting

00:15:16.901 --> 00:15:18.301
and destruction of the citizens there.

00:15:22.843 --> 00:15:26.864
At the time that was very controversial.

00:15:30.288 --> 00:15:34.009
And there was a lot of unhappiness that AP
was showing both sides of the fighting.

00:15:40.251 --> 00:15:43.692
Different people in those situations,
whether it's the U.S.

00:15:43.752 --> 00:15:47.333
military or the contracting companies or
the locals or a resistance group or

00:15:47.353 --> 00:15:50.574
whatever, are going to all perceive us

00:15:50.995 --> 00:15:54.716
in their own ways, and usually that means
with suspicion and basically seeing us as

00:15:54.717 --> 00:15:55.417
a problem.

00:15:57.356 --> 00:15:59.998
It did not stop us because that was the
norms at the time.

00:16:00.679 --> 00:16:02.540
I didn't know, I definitely didn't know
better.

00:16:02.740 --> 00:16:04.642
I thought it comes, it's a package.

00:16:05.503 --> 00:16:10.987
Fulfilling the dream of, you know, being a
mouthpiece for the Iraqis and getting the

00:16:11.868 --> 00:16:12.568
danger

00:16:12.668 --> 00:16:13.368
that comes with it.

00:16:15.230 --> 00:16:20.134
In recent and ongoing conflicts like Iraq
and Afghanistan, the highest death toll,

00:16:20.255 --> 00:16:21.736
if you like, is indigenous journalists.

00:16:22.224 --> 00:16:23.265
Particularly in the case of Iraq.

00:16:24.506 --> 00:16:29.269
We've had a black vehicle, surrounded
right to the north of the wall.

00:16:29.270 --> 00:16:34.674
We've lost colleagues who were local
journalists, filming alongside or close to

00:16:34.754 --> 00:16:35.454
insurgents.

00:16:35.454 --> 00:16:38.276
We've got a target 15 coming at you.

00:16:38.336 --> 00:16:44.641
On the battlefield, in the heat of the
moment, someone who looks different from

00:16:44.981 --> 00:16:45.681
the

00:16:45.681 --> 00:16:48.184
model of a foreign correspondent is even
more at risk.

00:16:48.608 --> 00:16:52.691
Because even if he is carrying a camera,
even if he is carrying a tripod, the

00:16:52.711 --> 00:16:55.573
assumption seems to be that they could be

00:16:55.633 --> 00:16:56.333
hostile.

00:16:56.333 --> 00:16:57.314
That's a weapon.

00:17:00.036 --> 00:17:05.680
What happened in 2007 to the two war news
journalists in Iraq was a terrible tragedy

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:07.001
and one that was brought

00:17:07.221 --> 00:17:10.923
home to all of us when we saw the footage
that WikiLeaks released.

00:17:11.304 --> 00:17:14.986
And I think that really brought home the
horror of modern war and why it is so

00:17:15.106 --> 00:17:15.806
dangerous.

00:17:16.307 --> 00:17:17.508
Yep, he's got a weapon too.

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:24.042
Hotel 26, Crazy Horse 185 to 6 individuals
with AK-47s.

00:17:24.043 --> 00:17:25.382
Request permission to engage.

00:17:25.402 --> 00:17:31.244
What you had there was Apache helicopters
firing on a group of people from at least

00:17:31.344 --> 00:17:32.264
500 meters away,

00:17:32.384 --> 00:17:34.585
maybe a couple of kilometers away.

00:17:35.045 --> 00:17:39.866
You have the assumption that everyone in
that group is of a type.

00:17:40.326 --> 00:17:45.067
You have the assumption that because there
were weapons in that group, that everyone

00:17:45.107 --> 00:17:47.308
in that group was armed and somehow
dangerous.

00:17:48.152 --> 00:17:53.574
It became just a terrible tragedy that the
gunships fired on those people.

00:17:54.254 --> 00:17:56.954
Our correspondents were killed along with
others.

00:18:13.579 --> 00:18:16.920
We need answers to the questions about how
these situations happen.

00:18:17.500 --> 00:18:22.623
We've engaged and will continue to engage
very actively with the authorities because

00:18:22.624 --> 00:18:24.465
we don't take for granted that people just
happen

00:18:24.485 --> 00:18:25.906
to be in the wrong place at the wrong
time.

00:18:26.046 --> 00:18:28.868
They were walking with a group where there
were arms.

00:18:29.228 --> 00:18:32.330
Now, in 2007, in Baghdad, that was
extremely...

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:33.130
common.

00:18:33.130 --> 00:18:34.251
Everyone was walking around with arms.

00:18:34.551 --> 00:18:38.694
But it became clear in the course of
investigating this that because they were

00:18:38.734 --> 00:18:41.416
walking with this group, they were assumed
to be

00:18:41.556 --> 00:18:42.256
hostile.

00:18:42.256 --> 00:18:47.280
So we changed our rules and said, don't go
anywhere near anybody who is carrying a

00:18:47.300 --> 00:18:48.000
weapon.

00:18:48.060 --> 00:18:51.963
Now that makes it more difficult to do
your job as a journalist, especially in a

00:18:51.983 --> 00:18:55.004
place where everyone is armed, but we felt
it

00:18:55.005 --> 00:18:56.505
was a necessary precaution to take.

00:19:20.949 --> 00:19:24.731
We were invited to an interview in
November of 2008 with a Taliban commander

00:19:24.772 --> 00:19:27.253
just outside of Kabul, the capital of
Afghanistan.

00:19:29.261 --> 00:19:33.106
The local journalist I was with had
interviewed this guy before.

00:19:33.507 --> 00:19:37.552
He had done the interviews with these two
European journalists, and he had not done

00:19:37.573 --> 00:19:38.273
anything.

00:19:38.273 --> 00:19:42.018
And so they had this relationship, and he
felt it would be okay.

00:19:45.049 --> 00:19:48.673
The issue of being taken hostage is a very
tricky one.

00:19:49.032 --> 00:19:53.596
I mean there are conventional ways to, if
you like, avoid being a target.

00:19:54.716 --> 00:19:57.578
But at the end of the day, if someone's
determined to take you hostage, then

00:19:57.920 --> 00:19:58.859
that's going to happen.

00:20:00.662 --> 00:20:04.364
We were told to drive to a meeting point
where we would meet these men who would

00:20:04.404 --> 00:20:05.345
take us to this interview.

00:20:10.933 --> 00:20:14.937
We rounded a corner and there was a car
blocking the road and these two Taliban

00:20:14.997 --> 00:20:16.778
guys rushed up to our vehicle.

00:20:18.439 --> 00:20:20.481
It didn't seem real at first.

00:20:20.621 --> 00:20:22.142
We thought there was some confusion.

00:20:22.221 --> 00:20:25.624
We hoped this was sort of a rough
introduction for our interview.

00:20:26.125 --> 00:20:28.567
But it became very clear that wasn't true.

00:20:41.717 --> 00:20:45.519
The key aspect of David's kidnapping was
most of the drama was played out in

00:20:45.558 --> 00:20:48.701
Waziristan, which is the absolute bastion
of

00:20:48.820 --> 00:20:52.801
the armed militant groups of the jihad in
Afghanistan.

00:20:55.923 --> 00:21:00.724
The initial demands in my case were for
$25 million in cash and the release of 15

00:21:00.764 --> 00:21:02.366
prisoners from Guantanamo Bay, Cuba.

00:21:03.145 --> 00:21:06.587
There's no kind of clear rules on what to
do in a kidnapping.

00:21:07.208 --> 00:21:09.249
And, you know, we survived, so I guess we
did the right thing.

00:21:09.328 --> 00:21:14.912
But the idea that these militants are
going to somehow have a sort of reasonable

00:21:14.991 --> 00:21:16.192
way of resolving the case

00:21:16.573 --> 00:21:17.374
doesn't apply.

00:21:17.894 --> 00:21:21.796
And we spent the next seven months in
captivity in north and south Waziristan,

00:21:22.356 --> 00:21:23.557
shuttled from house to house.

00:21:24.297 --> 00:21:28.319
And we were able to finally escape from
captivity in June of 2009.

00:21:28.321 --> 00:21:30.321
And we made it to a Pakistani military
base.

00:21:30.800 --> 00:21:32.541
And then we're allowed to go home.

00:21:41.969 --> 00:21:46.291
The people that are most vulnerable to
kidnapping are the local reporters who are

00:21:46.952 --> 00:21:48.713
doing very valuable

00:21:48.754 --> 00:21:50.336
reporting in their own locality.

00:21:50.516 --> 00:21:52.718
They are very, very much at risk.

00:21:53.761 --> 00:21:56.644
There are some differences between the
threats to the national journalists and

00:21:56.644 --> 00:21:57.664
the international journalists.

00:21:58.365 --> 00:22:02.767
In terms of the perceived rewards,
somebody who's conducting a kidnapping,

00:22:03.126 --> 00:22:04.948
they expect to get more out of it

00:22:05.328 --> 00:22:07.210
if they get hold of an international
journalist.

00:22:09.611 --> 00:22:13.313
But of course, if they just want to be
able to pick up a national journalist,

00:22:13.314 --> 00:22:16.015
then probably nobody will come after them
and they don't have to perceive any

00:22:16.055 --> 00:22:16.755
particular

00:22:16.755 --> 00:22:18.556
threat of law enforcement action.

00:22:22.693 --> 00:22:26.716
I am from Helmand.

00:22:26.757 --> 00:22:28.878
I am a member of the Taliban.

00:22:28.898 --> 00:22:29.598
I am a member of the

00:22:29.598 --> 00:22:37.404
Taliban.

00:22:37.405 --> 00:22:38.786
I am a member of the Taliban.

00:22:40.615 --> 00:22:43.336
I don't know if it's a good idea or not.

00:22:43.356 --> 00:22:45.195
I don't know if it's a good idea or not.

00:23:25.670 --> 00:23:31.673
I've been detained twice but I did feel
that going into Bosnian Serb territory and

00:23:31.692 --> 00:23:32.614
being arrested there was

00:23:32.673 --> 00:23:33.374
worth it.

00:23:34.954 --> 00:23:38.356
Journalist restrictions were simply that
we were not allowed into Bosnian Serb

00:23:38.417 --> 00:23:39.718
territory without an escort.

00:23:41.046 --> 00:23:45.009
I felt that they were hiding, you know,
this evidence of thousands of people

00:23:45.048 --> 00:23:45.749
dying.

00:23:45.890 --> 00:23:48.952
I went to checkpoints and said I was
simply driving through Bosnian Serb

00:23:48.991 --> 00:23:51.874
territory when in truth I was trying to
find these graves.

00:23:55.116 --> 00:23:59.380
Those stories helped prove that 8,000 men
and boys were executed in Srebrenica.

00:24:03.462 --> 00:24:07.226
A Bosnian Serb watchman saw me and
arrested me at the site.

00:24:08.026 --> 00:24:09.227
And I was held for 10 days.

00:24:11.386 --> 00:24:14.948
I was treated well, I was never beaten,
but they were convinced I was a spy.

00:24:19.610 --> 00:24:22.769
I think my kidnapping in Bosnia was
basically about controlling the message.

00:24:22.850 --> 00:24:25.851
They sort of kept denying that they had
carried out these executions.

00:24:26.452 --> 00:24:29.652
Right before I was released, they had me
do an interview with Bosnian Serb TV where

00:24:29.672 --> 00:24:31.533
I said I found no mass graves.

00:24:31.752 --> 00:24:34.014
You know, it was a false statement, but I
was desperate.

00:24:35.642 --> 00:24:41.205
I am very happy to be in Belgrade and very
grateful to the assistance of the Serbian

00:24:41.266 --> 00:24:42.547
government in getting me here.

00:24:42.548 --> 00:24:47.129
And it's a great relief for me to be able
to report to the Serbs.

00:24:47.411 --> 00:24:51.654
When journalists stop going to these
places, the militants that are terrorizing

00:24:51.673 --> 00:24:52.614
civilians, they win.

00:24:53.115 --> 00:24:56.456
The governments that are oppressing
opposition groups, they win.

00:25:04.486 --> 00:25:08.207
Our job is to report the world and to tell
the rest of the world what's happening and

00:25:08.208 --> 00:25:10.408
to shine light in the darkest corners of
the world.

00:25:10.929 --> 00:25:15.430
But we have to go on working very hard to
find the right way of doing that, the safe

00:25:15.490 --> 00:25:16.931
way of doing that, that doesn't put

00:25:17.571 --> 00:25:20.232
journalists and news teams' lives
unnecessarily at risk.

00:25:30.040 --> 00:25:32.444
The president is a liar The president is a
liar

00:25:55.903 --> 00:25:59.764
Why can anyone really think that our
calling as journalists is any less

00:25:59.804 --> 00:26:01.345
important than the calling of a soldier?

00:26:01.825 --> 00:26:02.625
I don't believe it is.

00:26:06.349 --> 00:26:10.553
The sooner we understand and manage that,
the sooner we're actually going to come up

00:26:10.554 --> 00:26:12.255
with safety policies that are really
effective.

00:26:26.981 --> 00:26:30.164
A journalist has to be concerned about all
the contingencies that could arise from

00:26:30.243 --> 00:26:33.926
landmines, to the possibility of
crossfire, to the possibility

00:26:33.927 --> 00:26:38.148
of incoming mortars, to military forces
simply changing sides.

00:26:38.470 --> 00:26:41.892
So a journalist needs a certain level of
combat awareness training.

00:26:42.031 --> 00:26:43.894
as well as emergency first aid.

00:26:47.338 --> 00:26:51.524
When the challenge started, I don't really
remember much after that.

00:26:52.444 --> 00:26:54.346
There were a couple of close-ups.

00:26:56.269 --> 00:26:56.969
And then we took...

00:26:56.969 --> 00:26:58.071
I took a direct hit somewhere.

00:26:58.471 --> 00:26:59.492
I was injured in the leg.

00:26:59.572 --> 00:27:05.298
I think my colleague Marie-Kelvin is dead,
and Remy also is dead, as far as I know.

00:27:06.178 --> 00:27:07.439
I don't know much at the moment.

00:27:09.241 --> 00:27:14.726
As long as people are fighting each other
and killing each other and doing so in the

00:27:14.786 --> 00:27:15.827
acquisition of power

00:27:16.628 --> 00:27:22.793
or territory or, you know, a point of
view, journalists have to be there.

00:27:26.746 --> 00:27:29.927
We have seen a rise in this culture of
impunity in some of the world's most

00:27:29.967 --> 00:27:33.588
dangerous countries, not talking about war
zones now, but countries like

00:27:33.748 --> 00:27:37.129
Mexico, where to kill a journalist is easy
censorship.

00:27:37.369 --> 00:27:41.350
So journalists who make themselves
awkward, who do investigative work, who

00:27:41.370 --> 00:27:43.911
get on the wrong side of particular

00:27:44.391 --> 00:27:48.932
crime gangs or even particular political
divisions, make themselves vulnerable.

00:27:49.172 --> 00:27:51.793
And we've seen that rise inexorably again
over the last decade.

00:28:21.914 --> 00:28:24.914
You feel a certain insecurity, right?

00:28:24.915 --> 00:28:29.077
Because it's dangerous to be a reporter
here in Mexico.

00:28:29.237 --> 00:28:34.460
International journalism organizations
have said that it's more dangerous to be a

00:28:34.461 --> 00:28:36.080
journalist in Mexico than in Iraq itself.

00:28:40.018 --> 00:28:45.342
What you hear are gunfire shots, rifle
caliber, and there is also even

00:28:46.503 --> 00:28:52.467
stronger sound than is happening on this
border of Reynosa.

00:28:52.768 --> 00:28:59.272
The confrontation between federal forces
and what is practically some

00:28:59.413 --> 00:29:01.294
organized cell of the military.

00:29:02.644 --> 00:29:08.717
Unfortunately, in many cases in these
regions, authorities are co-opted by

00:29:09.238 --> 00:29:09.938
criminal

00:29:09.938 --> 00:29:10.438
organizations.

00:29:11.223 --> 00:29:18.192
The influence of the cartels in many of
these areas is huge over law enforcement,

00:29:18.372 --> 00:29:20.315
politicians, and state prosecutors.

00:29:20.715 --> 00:29:26.282
And in most cases, these people are in
charge of investigating these crimes.

00:29:26.883 --> 00:29:29.446
Cartels cannot exist without the help of
the state.

00:29:30.147 --> 00:29:34.530
The police are the ones who give them a
way, the ones who stop investigating, the

00:29:34.531 --> 00:29:37.092
ones who provide them with vehicles, the
ones who provide them with

00:29:37.093 --> 00:29:37.793
logistics.

00:29:37.793 --> 00:29:41.314
And that's what affects journalists at
this moment.

00:29:46.607 --> 00:29:52.934
There are people who benefit from crime,
either drug barons or politicians, who

00:29:53.194 --> 00:29:53.894
want to

00:29:53.894 --> 00:29:56.858
send out a very strident signal to people
in our profession.

00:29:57.118 --> 00:29:59.280
They're targeting investigative reporting.

00:29:59.701 --> 00:30:03.445
They're targeting journalists who get up
every morning and want to spend time on

00:30:03.465 --> 00:30:04.686
investigating corruption.

00:30:07.407 --> 00:30:12.893
Fear is one of the overriding
characteristics affecting journalism in

00:30:12.953 --> 00:30:13.653
Mexico.

00:30:14.134 --> 00:30:19.079
Drug cartels have been basically
conducting war against journalists, as

00:30:19.119 --> 00:30:20.681
well as others in the community.

00:31:38.879 --> 00:31:44.663
Pursuing what was not merely my job but my
passion, I exceeded sheer reason on many

00:31:44.723 --> 00:31:45.423
occasions.

00:31:46.164 --> 00:31:50.728
So it often happened that in trying to
capture the best image or dig up a scoop,

00:31:51.648 --> 00:31:52.869
I crossed the line into

00:31:52.909 --> 00:31:53.609
danger.

00:31:53.930 --> 00:31:58.593
In 2006, I received reliable information
that several journalists were named on an

00:31:58.854 --> 00:32:00.655
organized crime hit list because of their

00:32:00.695 --> 00:32:02.356
reporting on the Juarez drug worse.

00:32:03.177 --> 00:32:05.278
My sword told me I wasn't that lazy.

00:32:07.115 --> 00:32:12.239
I was doing some investigation related
with these abuses, human rights abuses for

00:32:12.420 --> 00:32:14.041
militaries, but at the same

00:32:14.081 --> 00:32:18.084
time this incident happened at the
massacre of the rehab center.

00:32:19.065 --> 00:32:25.270
So I got information that witnesses said
that at least one big truck with soldiers

00:32:25.770 --> 00:32:32.496
blocked the road to help the hitmen to be
at the rehab center and kill these

00:32:32.676 --> 00:32:33.376
people.

00:32:33.396 --> 00:32:36.339
with these two investigations going side
by side.

00:32:37.003 --> 00:32:39.885
I started to notice that I had been
followed.

00:32:40.645 --> 00:32:46.468
One day my wife, she went back home and
outside of his minivan,

00:32:47.709 --> 00:32:53.012
she noticed that another minivan who was
following her parked just outside of the

00:32:53.052 --> 00:32:53.752
house.

00:32:55.273 --> 00:32:56.854
There's two men in the minivan.

00:32:58.435 --> 00:33:04.939
One of the men took out his arm and
pointed like this.

00:33:07.535 --> 00:33:14.220
This moment is when you understand that
your family, now they are in your

00:33:14.301 --> 00:33:15.001
danger.

00:33:17.463 --> 00:33:21.146
We have a phrase, it's quite common among
the police officers in Mexico.

00:33:21.887 --> 00:33:27.051
They say that the heart and your stomach
gives you good advices.

00:33:27.771 --> 00:33:29.453
So you have to learn to listen to them.

00:33:30.193 --> 00:33:33.056
So you feel that something is wrong.

00:33:34.517 --> 00:33:36.518
I can't explain it, how do you feel or...

00:33:38.131 --> 00:33:41.136
When do you feel that something is strange
there?

00:33:41.897 --> 00:33:45.243
So that's when I start to say, OK, I need
to leave.

00:33:54.255 --> 00:33:59.697
According to CBJ research, more than 85%
of crimes against journalists go unsolved.

00:34:00.297 --> 00:34:06.298
It's a huge problem which in many cases
perpetuates a climate of fear and

00:34:06.299 --> 00:34:06.999
intimidation

00:34:07.179 --> 00:34:10.039
where journalists and media are wide open
to attacks.

00:34:11.380 --> 00:34:17.902
Impunity is a green light for those who
attack journalists in places around the

00:34:17.903 --> 00:34:18.603
world.

00:34:25.685 --> 00:34:27.646
I've been doing this job for over 20
years.

00:34:27.706 --> 00:34:33.429
It's my passion and I have to keep

00:34:33.449 --> 00:34:34.889
going.

00:34:34.890 --> 00:34:37.551
I can't stay silent about what's going on.

00:35:41.398 --> 00:35:43.099
Intimidation goes the whole scale.

00:35:43.400 --> 00:35:47.463
I think you have to look on a case-by-case
basis whether the journalist is simply

00:35:47.843 --> 00:35:50.085
afraid of losing his livelihood or whether
that

00:35:50.105 --> 00:35:53.868
journalist is afraid of losing a limb or a
family member or his life.

00:35:54.369 --> 00:35:55.570
And all these things have happened.

00:35:55.810 --> 00:35:57.171
All these things are happening.

00:35:57.451 --> 00:36:01.134
All these things are very much real for
people in the journalistic community.

00:36:04.317 --> 00:36:09.541
Usually, they call you, they send you a
message through some...

00:36:11.230 --> 00:36:16.014
Even for a journalist, another journalist,
or a friend, or a lawyer, they call you

00:36:16.074 --> 00:36:17.635
and they say, you know what, you need to

00:36:18.636 --> 00:36:20.218
stay out of this area.

00:36:25.222 --> 00:36:32.167
In Tijuana, in the late 80s, early 90s,
the Arellano-Felix brothers were

00:36:32.168 --> 00:36:36.571
doing business in an open way, they were
recruiting young people from society in an

00:36:36.572 --> 00:36:37.272
open way.

00:36:43.566 --> 00:36:46.670
Don Jesús Blanco Ornelas was doing
research on organized crime.

00:36:46.930 --> 00:36:52.517
And when Blanco Ornelas identifies this
group as criminals or drug traffickers who

00:36:52.537 --> 00:36:53.899
are taking over the streets of the

00:36:53.919 --> 00:36:55.902
city, he starts pointing them out.

00:36:55.942 --> 00:37:02.250
And he forces the authorities to
investigate.

00:37:06.234 --> 00:37:10.957
The Arellano Felix brothers kill
Blancornelas, as a result of what we had

00:37:11.278 --> 00:37:12.679
published in the weekly newspaper.

00:37:12.739 --> 00:37:17.162
They kill Luis Valero, who was his
assistant and driver.

00:37:17.182 --> 00:37:20.644
And Don Jesus miraculously survives.

00:37:24.127 --> 00:37:27.069
The Don Jesus truck had more than 80
bullet impacts.

00:37:28.790 --> 00:37:32.913
Instead of the events of the attack, one
of the hitmen is killed.

00:37:32.914 --> 00:37:36.735
In fact, the one who was leading the group
of murderers.

00:37:36.875 --> 00:37:43.459
So, in our investigations, we realize that
this person belonged to the Logan

00:37:43.499 --> 00:37:48.703
neighborhood of San Diego and served in
the Arayano-Felix cartel.

00:37:48.843 --> 00:37:53.125
So, it was the main league to give to the
intellectuals of the crime.

00:37:54.798 --> 00:38:00.361
Why, if we know that the leaders of the
Cartel, Arayana Félix, attacked the life

00:38:00.362 --> 00:38:01.621
of Don Jesús Blanco Ornelas,

00:38:01.641 --> 00:38:04.163
why aren't they being judged for that
crime?

00:38:05.964 --> 00:38:10.626
Not many media, despite the fact that drug
trafficking is getting stronger and more

00:38:10.627 --> 00:38:12.767
terrible in Mexico and is causing

00:38:12.807 --> 00:38:18.530
more harm to Mexican society, dare to
investigate drug trafficking, organized

00:38:18.550 --> 00:38:19.250
crime.

00:38:19.470 --> 00:38:21.031
Precisely because of the consequences.

00:38:21.812 --> 00:38:22.512
What

00:38:22.512 --> 00:38:29.036
happened?

00:38:29.117 --> 00:38:30.878
What is the problem?

00:38:30.879 --> 00:38:31.579
What happened?

00:38:33.479 --> 00:38:36.462
Calm down In late July, four journalists
were kidnapped.

00:38:36.782 --> 00:38:39.725
Two of them worked for national TV
stations.

00:38:40.365 --> 00:38:46.811
And as a demand for the release, the
cartel said that the national TV must

00:38:47.471 --> 00:38:53.716
broadcast some videos showing the other
cartel in collusion with federal

00:38:53.997 --> 00:38:54.697
authorities.

00:39:06.729 --> 00:39:13.353
So the drug war in Mexico is not only
fought in the streets, it's also a war for

00:39:13.354 --> 00:39:14.054
the

00:39:14.054 --> 00:39:14.953
control of information.

00:39:17.815 --> 00:39:23.778
And there are indications now that the
cartels are trying to influence the

00:39:24.539 --> 00:39:26.900
information agenda more widespread.

00:39:28.960 --> 00:39:34.662
Cartels are telling publishers, editors
and reporters what they can publish or

00:39:34.882 --> 00:39:35.642
what they can't

00:39:35.722 --> 00:39:36.422
publish.

00:39:42.304 --> 00:39:44.704
The sense of impunity against press is
high now.

00:39:44.924 --> 00:39:49.046
Many people who can pay for kill a
journalist, maybe some of them are

00:39:49.066 --> 00:39:51.766
thinking in killing a journalist because

00:39:51.806 --> 00:39:53.487
it looks like nothing is going to happen.

00:39:55.247 --> 00:39:56.668
I think they have a double purpose.

00:39:56.828 --> 00:39:57.528
One is to...

00:39:58.184 --> 00:40:04.907
Shut up this person to eliminate this
person as well as to send

00:40:05.067 --> 00:40:06.748
the message to other journalists.

00:40:07.528 --> 00:40:09.669
This is going to happen if you don't
follow my rules.

00:40:33.227 --> 00:40:39.611
In 2004, my partner Francisco Javier Ortiz
Franco was murdered, also at the hands of

00:40:39.791 --> 00:40:40.491
the

00:40:40.491 --> 00:40:42.112
Arayano-Felix Cartel.

00:40:42.132 --> 00:40:48.896
He had just made a report in Z, about how
the structure of the

00:40:48.956 --> 00:40:54.179
cartel was integrated, and how they had a
relationship of complicity with the State

00:40:54.180 --> 00:40:55.200
Prosecutor's Office.

00:40:56.184 --> 00:41:00.671
I think it was one of the things that most
affected Don Jesús' life, obviously after

00:41:00.711 --> 00:41:01.752
his attack.

00:41:01.753 --> 00:41:07.100
Because he even thought that maybe it was
necessary to close Z, that how many more

00:41:07.101 --> 00:41:08.322
lives would he

00:41:08.742 --> 00:41:12.528
want to understand that in Mexico he
couldn't do what we were doing.

00:41:14.364 --> 00:41:17.868
The journalists themselves have tried to
get the government to pass laws saying,

00:41:17.888 --> 00:41:20.731
you know, a crime against a journalist is
a

00:41:21.251 --> 00:41:21.951
federal crime.

00:41:22.512 --> 00:41:28.739
But this has all been very ineffectual and
so the journalists have to be very careful

00:41:29.179 --> 00:41:29.879
what kind

00:41:29.879 --> 00:41:32.383
of information they're revealing about the
drug cartels.

00:41:36.068 --> 00:41:39.671
We have newspapers that are investigating
drug trafficking in Mexico.

00:41:40.451 --> 00:41:45.955
Of course, the life of a journalist
affects not only the family of that

00:41:45.956 --> 00:41:47.176
journalist or the

00:41:47.256 --> 00:41:50.979
media in which that journalist worked, but
many others.

00:42:03.228 --> 00:42:09.754
Censorship in Mexico is basically a result
of the threat of

00:42:09.794 --> 00:42:16.019
violence imposed by the drug cartels and
the failure of the government to provide

00:42:16.180 --> 00:42:16.880
minimal

00:42:16.880 --> 00:42:20.383
safety guarantees for reporters and the
media to do the work without fear of

00:42:20.403 --> 00:42:21.103
reprisal.

00:42:22.385 --> 00:42:25.028
Killing of journalists is not collateral
damage.

00:42:27.544 --> 00:42:32.487
The government is being complicit in not
investigating the murderers of

00:42:32.507 --> 00:42:33.207
journalists.

00:42:33.228 --> 00:42:39.312
The Mexican government has failed to
preserve freedom of expression in this

00:42:39.732 --> 00:42:40.432
country

00:42:40.432 --> 00:42:42.494
because sometimes freedom of expression is
uncomfortable for them.

00:42:58.473 --> 00:42:59.173
Stop recording.

00:42:59.173 --> 00:43:00.214
There was an accident.

00:43:00.454 --> 00:43:02.615
That's why there was an accident.

00:43:02.616 --> 00:43:03.316
I'm a communication man.

00:43:03.317 --> 00:43:04.316
I'm interested in accidents.

00:43:04.317 --> 00:43:05.017
Calm down.

00:43:05.017 --> 00:43:06.978
Don't get in my way again.

00:43:06.998 --> 00:43:08.559
If you want things to be good, don't do it
again.

00:43:08.579 --> 00:43:09.279
I'm going to stop recording.

00:43:09.280 --> 00:43:10.300
Of course I won't if this is live.

00:43:12.501 --> 00:43:16.483
Why don't you record the kidnappings we
do?

00:43:16.484 --> 00:43:17.184
Me too.

00:43:17.184 --> 00:43:20.986
What do you want to stop?

00:43:28.840 --> 00:43:35.767
At Z, we are very clear that our
commitment is to the readers, nothing

00:43:35.768 --> 00:43:36.468
more.

00:43:36.468 --> 00:43:40.312
We believe that no one, not the corrupt
government, not the abusive government,

00:43:40.313 --> 00:43:42.454
not the drug traffickers, not the
kidnappers, not the

00:43:42.494 --> 00:43:46.318
criminals, have the right to find a means
that society is supporting.

00:43:46.879 --> 00:43:50.102
Freedom, solidarity and the support we
have.

00:44:30.885 --> 00:44:35.328
Having seen the pervasive climate of
violent crime and impunity, I could not

00:44:35.368 --> 00:44:37.490
simply let myself be killed under

00:44:37.990 --> 00:44:39.411
some lonely streetlight.

00:44:40.980 --> 00:44:44.701
In September 2008, I left Mexico with my
family and went to Vancouver, Canada.

00:44:46.922 --> 00:44:49.063
I'm still alive and I'm fortunate for
that.

00:44:49.883 --> 00:44:52.744
But I feel the pain of having fled my
country and my profession.

00:44:54.084 --> 00:44:58.966
I now have a part-time job as a janitor,
the only work I could find after 14 months

00:44:58.967 --> 00:45:00.066
of unemployment.

00:45:01.587 --> 00:45:05.248
We are alive, out of the crossfire, but
having loved so much.

00:45:13.740 --> 00:45:14.508
Thank you.

00:45:26.676 --> 00:45:31.978
There have been 20 journalists murdered in
Russia since 2000 when President Putin

00:45:32.058 --> 00:45:33.219
came first to office.

00:45:33.419 --> 00:45:38.761
And in other of those cases have those who
commissioned the crimes been punished.

00:45:39.581 --> 00:45:45.484
Journalists are targeted for their work
when in the process of reporting

00:46:25.954 --> 00:46:29.895
The murders of journalists have become
less frequent in Russia.

00:46:30.475 --> 00:46:35.816
Attackers have favored severely injuring
and even maiming journalists on the job.

00:46:36.577 --> 00:46:43.198
One prominent example of this new and
frightful tendency is the case of

00:46:43.759 --> 00:46:45.659
journalist Mikhail Beketov.

00:46:48.940 --> 00:46:51.121
Of course, there were warnings.

00:46:51.181 --> 00:46:53.842
First, his dog was killed here in the
yard.

00:46:53.843 --> 00:46:56.323
Then the car was blown up.

00:46:56.324 --> 00:47:00.425
And the last one.

00:47:00.505 --> 00:47:06.668
The newspaper was called Khinkinskaya
Pravda.

00:47:07.088 --> 00:47:09.689
This newspaper was published twice a
month.

00:47:10.209 --> 00:47:14.931
Mikhail was the head of the department.

00:47:14.932 --> 00:47:15.632
He wrote the truth.

00:47:17.708 --> 00:47:23.853
He was expecting an attack from minute to
minute.

00:47:25.194 --> 00:47:26.115
But he made a

00:47:27.216 --> 00:47:33.021
miscalculation.

00:47:33.022 --> 00:47:35.964
Two killers were sent to Misha.

00:47:35.984 --> 00:47:39.407
They processed him right on the territory
of his house.

00:47:39.467 --> 00:47:40.167
They beat him.

00:47:41.056 --> 00:47:46.202
They broke his legs, his arms, his head.

00:47:46.242 --> 00:47:50.166
It's a miracle that he survived.

00:47:50.167 --> 00:47:53.730
He was lying there for days in the cold.

00:47:53.830 --> 00:48:00.036
And only because he was a soldier, a
paratrooper, a commander, and only because

00:48:00.116 --> 00:48:00.816
of his

00:48:00.816 --> 00:48:02.439
powerful health, did he survive.

00:48:04.160 --> 00:48:09.245
He was a towering figure before the
attack, and he had been long at odds with

00:48:09.246 --> 00:48:10.606
the local administration.

00:48:11.006 --> 00:48:15.690
He would not relent, and he paid the most
severe price.

00:48:16.691 --> 00:48:23.097
Even though he's alive now, he cannot do
his job anymore.

00:48:32.172 --> 00:48:35.142
When you were coming here, were you coming
from Himok?

00:48:37.230 --> 00:48:39.772
From the Khimki side, right?

00:48:39.812 --> 00:48:46.176
This is exactly the whole forest they
wanted to let go under the root.

00:48:46.177 --> 00:48:51.560
Khimki is a criminal region completely
under the control of the local

00:48:51.561 --> 00:48:52.261
authorities.

00:48:52.261 --> 00:48:52.761
In the

00:48:55.944 --> 00:48:59.806
city of Khimki, where the highway that is
built by Putin's friends and brothers in

00:48:59.807 --> 00:49:02.668
Attenberg with the help of the French
largest

00:49:02.688 --> 00:49:03.869
investment bank should pass.

00:49:05.346 --> 00:49:08.948
People stood up for the protection of the
forest.

00:49:08.968 --> 00:49:10.549
And we say that the highway can go to
another place.

00:49:13.750 --> 00:49:16.171
Vladimir Strebchenko is the mayor of the
city of Khimki.

00:49:17.152 --> 00:49:23.955
Apparently, he was interested in the sale
of land for the construction of this road.

00:49:23.995 --> 00:49:29.277
And everything is spinning from here.

00:49:43.610 --> 00:49:47.853
these terrible crimes against journalists
and so for years these crimes go

00:49:48.233 --> 00:49:49.414
uninvestigated and

00:49:49.594 --> 00:49:55.257
punished.

00:50:03.358 --> 00:50:06.281
Vladimir Putin is an ex-KGB officer.

00:50:06.341 --> 00:50:12.086
So there's a whole kind of shadowy world
of these operatives, some of whom, many of

00:50:12.087 --> 00:50:12.967
whom are now linked into

00:50:13.027 --> 00:50:15.890
business, some of whom have links with
organized crime.

00:50:17.271 --> 00:50:21.855
So if you want to do a business deal or
you want to take someone out, you have

00:50:22.195 --> 00:50:24.157
access to people who can do it for you.

00:50:31.763 --> 00:50:32.844
So they didn't have a client?

00:50:32.864 --> 00:50:36.246
There was a client, yes.

00:50:36.247 --> 00:50:40.248
And how do you think they will find a
client?

00:50:40.288 --> 00:50:42.069
Well, they say that the client is unknown.

00:50:47.531 --> 00:50:54.373
What is the state of the

00:50:54.533 --> 00:50:57.794
investigation?

00:50:57.814 --> 00:50:59.475
It's in a deadlock.

00:50:59.476 --> 00:51:02.576
Why can't they get the client?

00:51:02.636 --> 00:51:05.417
Because it's my own guess.

00:51:05.418 --> 00:51:08.538
There are high-ranking officials involved.

00:51:18.118 --> 00:51:23.566
Anna Politkovskaya was without a doubt
Russia's bravest investigative journalist

00:51:23.647 --> 00:51:24.908
but she was most famous for her

00:51:25.710 --> 00:51:27.452
reporting on Chechnya.

00:51:30.090 --> 00:51:32.791
At one point she was abducted there, but
then released.

00:51:32.792 --> 00:51:34.991
She was threatened several times.

00:51:35.692 --> 00:51:37.712
You have to be on the run all the time.

00:51:38.052 --> 00:51:41.893
Change clothes, stay alert, simply to do
your job as a journalist, to collect

00:51:41.953 --> 00:51:43.454
information from both sides.

00:51:43.854 --> 00:51:48.415
The kind of terrible irony is that she was
someone who faced death so many times in

00:51:48.435 --> 00:51:50.656
Chechnya, and then she was actually
murdered

00:51:50.816 --> 00:51:54.957
in the lobby of her apartment block in
Moscow.

00:51:54.997 --> 00:51:58.758
She was just stepping into the elevator
and someone surprised her and shot her.

00:52:00.726 --> 00:52:06.310
The case is being investigated, but they
are being

00:52:06.470 --> 00:52:10.112
investigated for murder.

00:52:10.152 --> 00:52:14.855
And as a rule, the conflict between the
journalist and the client is at the client

00:52:14.856 --> 00:52:16.196
level.

00:52:16.256 --> 00:52:19.819
And the client of this murder is not in
the case.

00:52:19.899 --> 00:52:26.623
The New Newspaper, the independent

00:52:26.783 --> 00:52:30.346
newspaper based in Moscow, is probably...

00:52:30.586 --> 00:52:37.312
the newspaper which knows most of what it
is like to lose colleagues to work-related

00:52:37.392 --> 00:52:38.092
murders.

00:52:58.439 --> 00:53:00.299
Natasha Estimirova was killed.

00:53:00.340 --> 00:53:01.560
She wrote a lot after her death.

00:53:01.680 --> 00:53:06.062
And now, in fact, Chechnya is a closed
zone for most of the

00:53:06.162 --> 00:53:11.325
readers.

00:53:13.606 --> 00:53:15.067
Nastya Baburova was killed.

00:53:15.068 --> 00:53:18.269
A young girl was shot.

00:53:18.989 --> 00:53:20.010
There was even a trainee in the staff.

00:53:24.066 --> 00:53:29.169
Stas Markilov, who has done many things in
the editorial department, also did

00:53:29.170 --> 00:53:30.350
anti-fascist cases, that is, he

00:53:30.410 --> 00:53:36.213
persecuted nationalists, racists.

00:53:36.214 --> 00:53:41.437
You have a top leadership in the country
that, while they give lip service to free

00:53:41.477 --> 00:53:43.158
expression, they really don't believe in

00:53:43.159 --> 00:53:43.859
it.

00:53:44.945 --> 00:53:50.769
When Vladimir Putin comes on the record
and says days after murder of Anna

00:53:50.829 --> 00:53:51.869
Politkovskaya that

00:53:51.970 --> 00:53:58.073
her role in society was insignificant,
when he says that her journalism didn't

00:53:58.134 --> 00:53:58.834
matter,

00:54:00.015 --> 00:54:06.179
that gives an extremely damaging message
that the journalism that she was doing

00:54:06.839 --> 00:54:07.680
doesn't matter.

00:54:10.937 --> 00:54:16.940
To this day we don't know who ordered the
gunman to her doorstep.

00:54:17.560 --> 00:54:20.481
People sort of were guessing pretty much
from the beginning who might be

00:54:20.501 --> 00:54:24.443
responsible, but it's very difficult to
pin these people down and it's very

00:54:24.583 --> 00:54:25.943
difficult to prove responsibility.

00:54:29.485 --> 00:54:36.208
I think that the hair of my correspondent
is not worth the facts that he will get.

00:54:36.248 --> 00:54:38.669
Apparently, it's time for me to leave this
job because...

00:54:40.337 --> 00:54:44.280
I am very afraid, and I am fairly afraid,
I have portraits hanging behind my back,

00:54:44.281 --> 00:54:47.142
of the risks for my people, and I explain
to

00:54:47.202 --> 00:54:53.026
everyone that no one's tears of your
parents are worth any material, even the

00:54:53.086 --> 00:54:54.167
most sensational one, that you will

00:54:54.168 --> 00:54:54.868
bring to the newspaper.

00:55:06.885 --> 00:55:11.087
Moscow police are investigating a brutal
attack on a correspondent with Russia's

00:55:11.147 --> 00:55:12.328
Kommersant newspaper.

00:55:12.868 --> 00:55:16.490
Oleg Kashin was fiercely beaten near his
home and is now in...

00:55:16.491 --> 00:55:22.012
I was speaking not as a investigator, but
as a commentator who called fools fools,

00:55:22.032 --> 00:55:22.732
and so on.

00:55:22.732 --> 00:55:27.175
It probably irritated many people and, in
principle, it was not such a big surprise

00:55:27.176 --> 00:55:27.876
for me.

00:55:27.876 --> 00:55:31.097
What story were you involved in when the
attack happened?

00:55:31.877 --> 00:55:33.518
There were several stories.

00:55:33.538 --> 00:55:38.520
The first one that came to my mind when I
woke up was the story of the Khinkin

00:55:38.521 --> 00:55:39.221
forest.

00:55:39.221 --> 00:55:43.782
It's a forest in and of itself, but
everyone doesn't care.

00:55:43.822 --> 00:55:47.424
All these painful points have come
together in this place.

00:55:47.464 --> 00:55:49.364
First of all, the environment is good in
Daugava.

00:55:49.404 --> 00:55:52.005
Secondly, it is the impudence, the
impudence and the impunity of the

00:55:52.006 --> 00:55:55.367
authorities, which are ready to go to
violence and deception to achieve their

00:55:55.368 --> 00:55:56.068
goals.

00:55:56.068 --> 00:55:56.247
Mr.

00:55:56.247 --> 00:56:02.091
Kashin is currently in a very critical
condition, in an induced coma.

00:56:02.471 --> 00:56:07.374
One video from a CCTV camera which
actually captured the attack itself leaked

00:56:07.474 --> 00:56:08.735
into the internet on Monday.

00:56:08.775 --> 00:56:10.196
The police have already strongly

00:56:11.857 --> 00:56:17.200
criticized...

00:56:18.481 --> 00:56:22.147
And I approach this cage, a man turns
around, looks at me, throws a bouquet on

00:56:22.148 --> 00:56:25.272
the ground and throws a very good boxer's
punch in the jaw, which I fall.

00:56:25.273 --> 00:56:31.162
I've already seen the rest on video,
because apparently I lost consciousness

00:56:31.222 --> 00:56:31.983
from the first blows.

00:56:34.217 --> 00:56:38.962
After that, the second man, whom I didn't
see, appeared from a dark corner with an

00:56:39.042 --> 00:56:40.564
iron rod in his hands.

00:56:40.565 --> 00:56:44.047
And with this rod he hit me, I tried to
count, but more than half a hundred hits

00:56:44.048 --> 00:56:45.729
on the head, especially on the mouth area.

00:56:45.769 --> 00:56:49.033
That is, my jaw was broken.

00:56:49.093 --> 00:56:53.618
And they deliberately broke my leg,
apparently, so that I wouldn't go

00:56:53.758 --> 00:56:54.458
anywhere.

00:57:00.437 --> 00:57:05.342
When I woke up, doctors told me that my
arm was bandaged, that one finger was

00:57:05.462 --> 00:57:07.344
amputated and another one

00:57:07.424 --> 00:57:08.124
was broken.

00:57:08.124 --> 00:57:10.928
Naturally, I had a romantic version that
they broke my finger so that I wouldn't

00:57:10.948 --> 00:57:11.648
write.

00:57:11.648 --> 00:57:17.895
We argue with colleagues, investigators,
whether they

00:57:17.915 --> 00:57:19.056
wanted to kill or not.

00:57:19.096 --> 00:57:22.360
Probably, they didn't want to, because if
they wanted to kill, they would have

00:57:22.440 --> 00:57:23.140
killed.

00:57:33.317 --> 00:57:37.541
The fact that my story caused a surge of
indignation among my colleagues, exactly

00:57:37.561 --> 00:57:40.023
such a unity, it was insanely surprising

00:57:40.223 --> 00:57:45.348
to me when I came to myself, because I
could not imagine that there was, at

00:57:45.349 --> 00:57:46.889
least, some solidarity among journalists.

00:57:46.929 --> 00:57:50.412
Irritation was accumulated, irritation,
indignation with all the murders.

00:57:56.709 --> 00:57:58.793
I think it's just a kind of natural
phenomenon.

00:57:59.375 --> 00:58:03.303
This generation, this class is standing up
for freedom of speech.

00:58:28.677 --> 00:58:35.344
Unfortunately, the problem of our
government It's not about our

00:58:35.345 --> 00:58:36.045
journalism.

00:58:36.184 --> 00:58:40.906
They don't need journalists for the
knowledge they have and for the leadership

00:58:40.946 --> 00:58:41.726
of the country.

00:58:41.766 --> 00:58:44.247
Show me the document Here I am showing you
the document.

00:58:44.248 --> 00:58:49.189
Press, Echo, Moscow and the Communist
Party.

00:58:55.012 --> 00:58:58.073
The role of the journalist is to provide
transparency, to tell what's really

00:58:58.133 --> 00:59:01.514
happening, to uncover the true story, to
cut behind the

00:59:01.515 --> 00:59:03.435
propaganda, to cut behind the government
line.

00:59:31.919 --> 00:59:37.402
The main goal of this publication was to
inform the population about the official

00:59:37.802 --> 00:59:38.603
madness.

00:59:40.564 --> 00:59:42.765
We protested against the increase in the
rent.

00:59:42.785 --> 00:59:47.407
After the rally ended, I asked people to
accompany us, because we were afraid that

00:59:47.508 --> 00:59:48.208
the

00:59:48.208 --> 00:59:51.570
protesters could behave inadequately.

00:59:54.427 --> 00:59:56.170
I was the last one to go.

00:59:56.290 --> 00:59:59.455
Three people came to me, they were in the
police station.

00:59:59.495 --> 01:00:01.598
Later we found out that they were police
officers.

01:00:01.638 --> 01:00:06.346
Without explaining the reason, they broke
my arms and started beating me.

01:00:19.771 --> 01:00:23.654
Luckily, there were just 3,000 witnesses
who were not indifferent.

01:00:23.774 --> 01:00:29.679
They just clung to the ring and began to
push the policemen away.

01:00:29.719 --> 01:00:32.281
Grandmothers with sticks were pushed away.

01:00:32.282 --> 01:00:33.041
Who could have done it?

01:00:33.042 --> 01:00:35.543
I lost consciousness several times,
because they hit me on the head, when they

01:00:35.583 --> 01:00:37.024
realized that they could not lead me away.

01:00:37.025 --> 01:00:38.265
But the most painful place

01:00:42.448 --> 01:00:43.148
is the head.

01:00:43.148 --> 01:00:45.190
It remains a question who beat me.

01:00:46.967 --> 01:00:49.189
These faces are still not officially
identified.

01:00:49.229 --> 01:00:54.293
Moreover, the police officer who attacked
me, the most important one who hit me on

01:00:54.294 --> 01:00:55.934
the head, got promoted and became deputy

01:00:55.994 --> 01:01:00.097
chief immediately after the incident with
my beating.

01:01:03.400 --> 01:01:09.244
Whenever enterprising journalists are
slain or silenced or drowned and

01:01:09.284 --> 01:01:10.225
marginalized,

01:01:11.206 --> 01:01:12.206
everybody suffers.

01:01:14.319 --> 01:01:20.504
Here's an example of an extremist case
that was filed against me.

01:01:20.565 --> 01:01:25.329
How many papers did I have to write?

01:01:25.330 --> 01:01:26.030
Three examinations.

01:01:26.030 --> 01:01:31.534
For journalists, the law allows them to be
removed from their activities by accusing

01:01:31.594 --> 01:01:32.355
them of

01:01:32.395 --> 01:01:33.095
extremism.

01:01:38.620 --> 01:01:39.601
says that in Russia...

01:01:39.681 --> 01:01:41.603
Russia is especially dangerous to be a
journalist.

01:01:41.863 --> 01:01:43.684
Russia is especially dangerous to be
anyone.

01:01:44.805 --> 01:01:46.547
I'm not going anywhere with you.

01:01:46.548 --> 01:01:48.849
I'm not going anywhere with you.

01:01:49.489 --> 01:01:51.811
Your actions are illegal.

01:01:51.831 --> 01:01:52.872
You are violating my rights.

01:01:52.992 --> 01:01:58.617
Extremism is called disagreement with the
opinion of the authorities expressed in

01:01:58.618 --> 01:01:59.318
any sharp form.

01:01:59.319 --> 01:02:05.002
Like six harmonies stretched to any width
and playing any melody necessary to power.

01:02:06.787 --> 01:02:10.830
If I was told in the last minute, don't
write about this and that, then you won't

01:02:10.831 --> 01:02:11.650
be able to get your head around it.

01:02:11.651 --> 01:02:16.714
I would probably say, go to hell, I would
still write about it, but in principle,

01:02:16.854 --> 01:02:17.915
it's just my

01:02:17.916 --> 01:02:21.357
stupidity and stupidity, because of
course...

01:02:21.497 --> 01:02:23.457
It's not worth it, definitely.

01:02:23.477 --> 01:02:26.498
I'd rather give myself back this memory
than write an extra note of truth.

01:02:26.499 --> 01:02:27.498
...and for the state, we can say, in such
a period...

01:02:32.419 --> 01:02:39.341
It's a measure of a civil society to have
an active, aggressive press.

01:02:39.941 --> 01:02:43.822
And when that starts to break down, civil
society starts to break down.

01:02:43.823 --> 01:02:48.283
...and now to honor the memory of our
comrades...

01:02:48.284 --> 01:02:48.984
Today we...

01:02:48.984 --> 01:02:52.120
Can we say that we live in a civil
society?

01:02:52.121 --> 01:02:53.121
Of course not.

01:02:53.161 --> 01:03:00.144
Our society is shattered today by people
who really

01:03:00.224 --> 01:03:05.686
want the progress of our country towards a
free Russia.

01:03:05.687 --> 01:03:11.369
And a huge number of people who are
pulling us back to Stalinism, to the

01:03:11.389 --> 01:03:15.911
darkest abyss of the Unliberation.

01:03:17.383 --> 01:03:20.085
It's clear to everyone.

01:03:20.185 --> 01:03:25.749
We will always say that we have democracy,
that our people rule the country.

01:03:26.129 --> 01:03:30.893
But if in our places there is such a
vokhanali, such madness of officials,

01:03:30.913 --> 01:03:32.594
corrupt officials, bandits,

01:03:33.575 --> 01:03:34.615
no one will respect us in the

01:03:34.795 --> 01:03:45.023
world.

01:03:45.363 --> 01:03:48.164
the investigative journalism is extinct.

01:03:48.984 --> 01:03:49.684
Not yet.

01:03:51.265 --> 01:03:54.566
But it is an endangered species.

01:03:55.726 --> 01:03:59.707
Only takes one journalist to be beaten or
assaulted or murdered for the rest of his

01:03:59.747 --> 01:04:01.347
colleagues to think, why am I in this
profession?

01:04:01.788 --> 01:04:03.768
I'm not being well compensated for this
profession.

01:04:04.408 --> 01:04:05.409
What keeps me going?

01:04:05.769 --> 01:04:09.390
Isn't there a easier way of doing, you
know, making a living?

01:04:11.190 --> 01:04:13.271
Now I have left the...

01:04:14.711 --> 01:04:18.493
Journalists, and, accordingly, in my
opinion, I do not represent a threat for

01:04:18.513 --> 01:04:19.213
them.

01:04:19.213 --> 01:04:23.095
Messages with threats have stopped coming.

01:04:23.215 --> 01:04:28.517
Thank God, no one tried to beat me up with
a car, and the police did not beat me up.

01:04:28.518 --> 01:04:33.799
If suddenly I become the editor-in-chief
again and start, God forbid, to condemn

01:04:33.959 --> 01:04:35.340
them again in all

01:04:35.380 --> 01:04:42.223
the sins, I think there will be the same
scenario.

01:04:43.451 --> 01:04:44.732
You see, I worry about my city.

01:04:44.733 --> 01:04:47.454
I don't want it to be clean and beautiful.

01:04:47.455 --> 01:04:51.196
I don't want to have enough space for all
children in kindergartens.

01:04:51.197 --> 01:04:54.038
I want to have roads without pits.

01:04:54.039 --> 01:04:55.259
I've always been indifferent.

01:04:55.319 --> 01:04:58.921
Unfortunately, it's impossible to work
here.

01:04:58.982 --> 01:05:00.382
It's very unreal in places so far.

01:05:00.443 --> 01:05:03.645
Do you have any ways to work for
journalists?

01:05:03.646 --> 01:05:04.346
Anonymously.

01:05:04.346 --> 01:05:04.866
How?

01:05:04.866 --> 01:05:11.650
Anonymously.

01:05:33.328 --> 01:05:36.611
I think the issue of journalist safety is
something which should concern everybody

01:05:36.651 --> 01:05:37.751
and not just journalists.

01:05:38.072 --> 01:05:40.473
It should concern everybody who cares
about the freedom of speech.

01:05:44.347 --> 01:05:48.528
If you could surface some of the bravery
and some of the courage that goes on in

01:05:48.548 --> 01:05:51.029
order to tell the world what's happening,
then I think that

01:05:51.329 --> 01:05:54.250
society would respond better to the
business of journalism and what we're

01:05:54.251 --> 01:05:54.951
trying to do.

01:05:57.371 --> 01:05:59.291
Certainly politicians think the media is
incredibly powerful.

01:05:59.292 --> 01:06:04.693
Why don't we use some of that power to
support those people who need it, to

01:06:04.733 --> 01:06:06.133
support the safety of those people who are

01:06:06.134 --> 01:06:10.814
taking all these risks, by ensuring that
there is a penalty more often than not for

01:06:10.834 --> 01:06:11.615
killing a journalist.

01:06:15.575 --> 01:06:20.041
So we have managed to persuade the United
Nations Security Council to sign up to

01:06:20.562 --> 01:06:21.262
article

01:06:21.262 --> 01:06:27.431
1738, which sends out a very strong signal
that you can't murder journalists

01:06:27.932 --> 01:06:28.974
and hope to get away with it.

01:06:32.079 --> 01:06:36.323
The Daniel Pearl Act was passed which said
that the US government really called on

01:06:36.683 --> 01:06:38.865
nations to protect journalists and not to

01:06:38.905 --> 01:06:40.226
allow something like that to happen.

01:06:40.706 --> 01:06:45.370
However, much like the UN resolution is a
statement of intent, it has no real force.

01:06:55.018 --> 01:06:58.761
Almost everyone I know who does this has
this sense of mission.

01:06:59.362 --> 01:07:00.242
If you are...

01:07:00.963 --> 01:07:05.168
in a war zone and see something happen in
front of you that shouldn't be happening.

01:07:06.049 --> 01:07:10.134
And you bring that back to where the
policymakers are and they see that and

01:07:10.135 --> 01:07:11.135
they change the policy.

01:07:12.150 --> 01:07:13.031
You are doing your job.

01:07:17.294 --> 01:07:18.055
I am optimistic.

01:07:18.075 --> 01:07:22.939
I think that you see spreading around the
world this general desire for the rule of

01:07:22.999 --> 01:07:24.781
law and for governments that are more

01:07:24.801 --> 01:07:25.862
accountable to their people.

01:07:26.562 --> 01:07:27.883
People want information.

01:07:27.903 --> 01:07:30.205
They want judicial systems that are
independent.

01:07:30.946 --> 01:07:35.570
As that continues, you will see that there
will be accountability for people who

01:07:36.410 --> 01:07:37.771
abuse journalists and less

01:07:37.832 --> 01:07:38.532
impunity.

01:07:51.765 --> 01:07:55.769
It's really common I think for people to
be looking for new sources of information

01:07:56.010 --> 01:07:58.753
and I think that that really underscores
and puts

01:07:58.793 --> 01:08:02.437
more importance on journalism and how
great the need is.

01:08:07.178 --> 01:08:09.199
It's like we need fresh air to breathe.

01:08:09.460 --> 01:08:14.323
You know, we need it, we know it when it's
not there, we suffer from its lack, and

01:08:14.603 --> 01:08:15.664
we're going to look for it.

01:08:17.805 --> 01:08:21.288
I always have a romantic vision of...

01:08:22.248 --> 01:08:25.989
I love my job, it's my passion, it's my
life.

01:08:25.990 --> 01:08:28.570
But I think our job is also about taking
risks.

01:08:28.610 --> 01:08:34.352
So we have to control it, we have to be
very careful, even more careful.

01:08:34.372 --> 01:08:41.115
But I don't think we can afford to tell a
country, we're not going there anymore,

01:08:41.155 --> 01:08:43.656
goodbye everyone, and we'll call you in a
few years when things get better, to

01:08:43.657 --> 01:08:45.957
interview those who have survived.

01:08:45.958 --> 01:08:47.777
We can't afford to do that when we're
journalists.

01:08:59.467 --> 01:09:00.808
I'm not sure if you can hear me.

01:09:22.454 --> 01:09:25.177
It's part of a conversation, it's now
leaning forward.

01:09:25.858 --> 01:09:29.863
Not only does it want to know about what's
going on, it wants to influence the

01:09:29.983 --> 01:09:30.683
outcome.

01:09:31.605 --> 01:09:37.452
So the most humble of person anywhere in
the world with a handheld telephone can

01:09:37.552 --> 01:09:38.433
play a part in that

01:09:38.493 --> 01:09:41.237
conversation and can influence the
outcome.

01:09:45.206 --> 01:09:48.628
Anywhere that there are journalists
working, I think, is actually good for

01:09:48.668 --> 01:09:51.810
democracy, good for society, good for the

01:09:51.830 --> 01:09:53.091
population at large.

01:09:55.553 --> 01:09:56.253
People need to know.

01:09:56.713 --> 01:09:58.254
People need to be able to make up their
own minds.

01:10:01.196 --> 01:10:04.318
What interests us when we say this is to
say that the press is us because we write,

01:10:04.378 --> 01:10:05.479
but it's you because you read it.

01:10:05.499 --> 01:10:09.361
And to defend the freedom of the press is
you to defend, it's not us to defend.

01:10:16.794 --> 01:10:22.237
Killing of journalists, it's not a
question that is directed to journalists.

01:10:22.918 --> 01:10:26.540
This is a question that is directed to
society.

01:10:29.642 --> 01:10:35.845
When we put something on the air that
helps someone who had no way to reach the

01:10:35.885 --> 01:10:36.585
levers of

01:10:36.585 --> 01:10:39.107
power, that's what the Fourth Estate is
all about.

01:10:39.908 --> 01:10:41.829
That's why I'm risking my life.

